  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 230 AM CST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nThis Weekend:\nAnother longwave trough digs into the region this weekend, with\nthe jet stream remaining displaced south of the area. Thus,\ndrier weather looks likely through the weekend as well. As the\ntrough drops into the Southern Plains, cooler air filters into\nthe area, with highs more seasonable in the middle/upper 50s to\nlower 60s. Meanwhile, this pattern will be accompanied by cooler\novernight temperatures, with widespread lows in the 30s expected\neach morning. The growing season continues into early November\nas captured in the latest MRCC Vegetation Freeze Susceptibility\nGuidance, showing all of the area as still susceptible. For \nthis weekend, lows in the middle 30s and calm overnight winds \nlook to set the stage for frost. However, the greatest potential\nfor lows below freezing are currently focused across the \neastern Ozarks, where lows in the upper 20s to lower 30s looks \nlikely. The potential for frost/freeze headlines will continue \nto be assessed as we approach this weekend.\n\nNext Week:\nAs we get into early next week, a ridge looks to take hold over\nthe central and western CONUS. As mid-level heights increase, as\nwill the temperatures once again into mid-November. This \nmatches up well with CPC's latest 6 to 10 and 8 to 14 day \noutlooks, highlighting increasing confidence in above normal \ntemperatures. NBM interquartile spreads continue to shrink, with\nhighs progged to return to the upper 60s to lower 70s. This is \nanywhere from 5 to 10+ degrees above normal for early to mid \nNovember. Drier conditions look to persist, which may amplify \nthe mid-Fall fire weather conditions.",
